{"id":"m2://rec/open_logic/content/computability/computability-theory/no-universal-function.tex#thm:no-univ#1","source":"open_logic","snapshot":"blake3:a5eb116529d3e799289dd9f7e7530d0d446b3615f5608fe08c668241eee3c765","locator":"https://github.com/OpenLogicProject/OpenLogic/blob/1e960beff9ed7835bf3e3f1335e21af3439cd107/content/computability/computability-theory/no-universal-function.tex#L16","native_id":"content/computability/computability-theory/no-universal-function.tex#thm:no-univ","kind":"statement","text":"There is no universal computable function. In other words, any\nfunction $\\fn{Un}'(k, x)$ which is such that if $f(x)$ is a total\ncomputable function, then there is a natural number~$k$ such that\n$f(x) = \\fn{Un}'(k,x)$ for every~$x$, is not computable.","class":"open","by":"textbook/1","resource":"PKGMM3P5QY","at":{"section":"3.8","kind":"thm"}}
